From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: a733: the display engine node, and the DE -> TCON_TV1 edge

The device-tree half of `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-display-engine-clock-gate-reset`. Three changes to one file:

  1. a mixer node -- the display engine, DE0 @0x05000000, with the four
     register windows a DE backend driver has to reach, its bus gate, its
     module clock, its reset and its interrupt;
  2. a ports/endpoint pair joining that node to tcon_tv1, because a graph edge
     only exists if both ends describe it;
  3. one comment moved back to the node it describes -- see the last section.

⚠ THIS HEADER WAS CORRECTED TWICE, AND THE CLOCK PARENT CHANGED. Its first
version pinned DE0_CLK to PERI0_480M on the grounds that PLL_DE feeds the SoC
interconnect and that the reset mux would hand the engine a stopped clock.
Readings on the vendor system with the display engine live, and then at the
U-Boot prompt on a cold power-cycle of our own chain, retired the first claim
and turned the second into an argument for the opposite conclusion. This node
now pins DEPLL3X -- the mux's reset value, and what the vendor uses. The old
reasoning is deleted rather than struck through; the new one is below.

WHAT THIS NODE IS FOR
---------------------
Everything on the DisplayPort path up to now generates its own picture:
TCON_TV1 has a built-in test-pattern generator (VDBIST) and that
is how the 2026-08-18 picture was produced with no display engine, no KMS and
no DRM device at all. The DE is the block that replaces the test pattern with
a framebuffer out of DRAM. Nothing binds this node today; the driver is being
written separately.

WHERE THE VALUES COME FROM -- AND WHERE THEY CANNOT
----------------------------------------------------
Documented, and cited:

  DE0 aperture   0x05000000-0x053FFFFF, 4 MiB, group DE_SYS
                 (A733 user manual memory map)
  no DE1         DE0 is the only display engine among the UM's 143 modules;
                 the feature list (up to 7 alpha-blending channels, two display
                 outputs; A733 user manual) describes two ports of ONE block
  interrupt      INTID 82 "DE" = GIC_SPI 50,
                 the A733 user manual -- one interrupt for the whole engine, no
                 per-channel and no per-output DE interrupt
  clocks/reset   CLK_BUS_DE0 / CLK_DE0 / RST_BUS_DE0, added by `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-display-engine-clock-gate-reset` from
                 DE0_BGR_REG 0x0A04 / abs 0x02002A04 bit0+bit16 and DE0_CLK_REG
                 0x0A00 / abs 0x02002A00

⛔ AND THE PART THAT IS NOT, WHICH GOVERNS THE REST. A733 UM V0.92 chapter 14.3
"Display Engine" is two pages of prose with no register list, no offset table
and no field table. Measured over the whole 2.7 MB text extract, not assumed:
zero hits for GLB_CTL, zero for BLD_, zero for OVL_, zero for blender, zero for
RCQ as anything but a single feature bullet. Compare TCON_TV,
which gets its own register chapter with per-bit tables, or eDP, which gets
15.4.7. So no offset INSIDE the aperture can carry a manual reference, and asking for one
is asking for a fabrication.

The evidence floor for the four windows is therefore a live capture -- 29 233
non-zero words read O_RDONLY off the 8 GiB board on 2026-08-18 while the vendor stack drove
a 1920x1200 four-lane DP link
-- plus block arithmetic that predicts where things sit, plus candidate names
from mainline's sun8i_mixer and from the out-of-tree A523/T527 sun55i_de series.
The evidence was compiled tag by tag from that capture. Two things about that method matter here:

  - a name from code is a HINT; it is kept only where the capture confirms it at
    that offset and dropped where the capture refuses it. Both outcomes occur in
    this evidence, which is what makes it a method rather than a transcription.
  - ⚠ the A523/T527 series is a LEAD, not a source. Treating the A523 as
    authoritative has caused errors here before. Every window below
    is INFERRED in exactly this sense and is labelled so; none of it is spec.

THE FOUR WINDOWS
----------------
The first three are mainline's DE33 mixer names and order, because that is the
register model this silicon answers to -- drivers/gpu/drm/sun4i/sun8i_mixer.c
takes reg index 0 for "layers" and then looks up "top" and "display" BY NAME, so
"layers" has to stay at index 0:

  layers   0x05100000 + 0x180000  the channel array. Channel n sits at
           0x05100000 + n * 0x20000; the capture shows seven populated slots on
           exactly that stride -- {0,1,2} and {6,7,8,9} -- against the UM's seven
           blend channels. Three independent things agree on
           that arithmetic, and one of them is our own silicon. The window runs
           up to where display pipe 0 begins, i.e. slots 0..11.
  top      0x05008100 + 0x40      mixer 0's per-pipe control block. Four
           consecutive registers decode as mainline's DE33 layout exactly:
           GLOBAL_CTL = 0x1 (RT_EN, what sun8i_mixer_init() writes),
           GLOBAL_STATUS, GLOBAL_SIZE = 0x04af077f = 1920x1200, GLOBAL_CLK = 1.
           The size is 0x40 because mixer 1's block begins at 0x05008140 -- and
           that address reads all zero across its whole 0x40 on a board with one
           display pipe up. A layout that predicts a second block at a specific
           address and finds it empty for the right reason is an identification,
           not a coincidence.
  display  0x05280000 + 0x20000   display pipe 0: the blender at +0x1000 (five
           registers, four instances, all four programmed identically on the
           live link) and the output formatter at +0x5000.
  de-top   0x05008000 + 0x100     the DE-global block below the per-mixer tops.
           DE2TCON_MUX at +0x10 (live 0x0000fff5) and the version word at +0x14
           (live 0x03520100 -> DE version 0x352, which the vendor DT string
           "allwinner,display-engine-v352" and the out-of-tree series' own
           "de352" independently agree on).

⚠ de-top IS A FOURTH WINDOW WITH NO COUNTERPART IN MAINLINE'S DE33 BINDING, and
it is deliberately in even though no driver reads it yet. Without it nothing can
write DE2TCON_MUX, and without that write the composed picture never reaches
TCON_TV1 -- the block would come up, report its version, accept a framebuffer
and put it nowhere. This is `arm64-dts-a733-tcon-tv1-and-the-dp-phy-pad-window`'s lesson applied before it costs a session:
that patch's first cut withheld a second reg window and a clock on the reasoning
that nothing had been shown to need them, and the driver had to announce the gap
at probe before anyone noticed. Withholding is not the safe default; it is the
expensive one.

⚠ WINDOW BOUNDARIES ARE WEAKER EVIDENCE THAN WINDOW BASES, and the difference is
worth stating rather than hiding behind four tidy numbers. Each base is confirmed
by a register that decodes at it. Each size comes from where the next block
starts, which is an inference from the block map. Falsified by: a live register
outside these four ranges that the datapath needs. The honest fix if that
happens is to map the whole 4 MiB in one window -- it costs nothing but address
space, and it is the
safe answer.

THE CLOCK PARENT: DEPLL3X, AND WHY THAT REVERSED
-------------------------------------------------
assigned-clock-parents pins DE0_CLK's seven-way mux to DEPLL3X. It is written
out rather than left to the reset default so that a boot chain which moved it
cannot change what this node means silently.

WHAT THE COLD BOARD HANDS US, read at the U-Boot prompt on a cold power-cycle of
our own chain, nothing having run:

    PLL_DE_CTRL abs 0x020022E0 = 0x41125600   PLL_EN 0 -- the PLL is OFF
    DE0_CLK     abs 0x02002A00 = 0x00000000   gate off, mux 000 = DEPLL3X
    DE0_BGR     abs 0x02002A04 = 0x00000000   bus gate off, reset asserted
    NSI_CLK     abs 0x02002580 = 0xC3000000   CLK_SEL 011 = PERI0_600M

WHAT THE VENDOR DOES, with its display engine genuinely live -- version word
0x05008014 = 0x03520100, VI scanout 0x05101018 = 0xFC000000, picture on the
monitor:

    PLL_DE_CTRL abs 0x020022E0 = 0xFD124A00   PLL_EN and LOCK set, N 75
    DE0_CLK     abs 0x02002A00 = 0x80000000   mux 000 = DEPLL3X, FACTOR_M 0,
                                              i.e. DIVISOR M = 1 -> 600 MHz

THE REVERSAL. This node first pinned PERI0_480M, on three grounds, and all three
are now deleted:

  - "PLL_DE feeds NSI, the SoC interconnect, so we must not own it." The UM row
    "PLL_DE: NSI, DE0, VE, NPU, DRAM0, CSI" is a fan-out map,
    not active topology: all six reach DEPLL3X through muxes with other inputs,
    and five were read on the live vendor system -- NSI -> PERI0_600M, DRAM0 abs
    0x02002C00 -> DDRPLL, VE_ENC0 abs 0x02002A80 -> VE1PLL, VE_DEC abs
    0x02002A88 -> VE0PLL, NPU abs 0x02002B00 -> NPUPLL. None on DEPLL3X. NSI
    reads the same value cold, so the answer does not depend on whose operating
    system is running. The sixth, CSI, has no driver and no node in this tree.
  - "the reset mux hands the engine a stopped clock." TRUE for our path, and
    that is now an argument for owning the PLL rather than avoiding it. Cold, the
    mux already points at DEPLL3X and DEPLL3X is off, so a driver that ungates
    DE0 and leaves the clock tree alone reads 0x00000000 out of the whole 4 MiB
    with its gate and reset both correct. There are exactly two ways out and no
    third: move the mux to a peripheral tap and never own the PLL, or keep the
    mux where the silicon puts it and own the PLL properly. With the interconnect
    hazard measured dead, the second describes the block as it is built.
  - "the UM tells a 2K mode to use another source". True as
    text, but it is the consequence of the sentence before it -- NSI at 700 MHz
    means DEPLL3X must not be retuned, so a 2K DE that wants less must look
    elsewhere. With NSI measured off DEPLL3X the premise does not hold here and
    the PLL can be tuned to what the mode needs, which is what the vendor does:
    600 MHz, not the reset 696.

WHAT PINNING DEPLL3X BUYS. The source the silicon defaults to and the vendor
uses, at the rate mainline's DE33 model also picks. The whole chain is settable,
so one clk_set_rate() on the module clock configures divider, tap and PLL. And
4K stays reachable -- 696 MHz is a PLL_DE rate and no PERI0 tap gets near it, so
PERI0_480M would have capped the engine 20 % below the vendor and ruled 4K out
permanently.

⚠ WHAT IT COSTS. This display path now owns PLL_DE: a modeset retunes it, and
clk_disable_unused() switches it off on a kernel where nothing claims it. Bounded
by the sweep above -- five consumers measured elsewhere, CSI absent from this
tree -- and it is the same bargain `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-video-plls-and-edp-tv-pixel-clock` struck for PLL_VIDEO1, whose header
records CSI_MASTER as inheriting it. A future VE, NPU or DRAM driver that selects
DEPLL3X from its own mux inherits a clock the display retunes.

There is no assigned-clock-rates: the rate belongs to a modeset, not to the
device tree. ⚠ Like the TCON's, these properties take effect when a driver binds
this node -- of_clk_set_defaults() runs from platform_probe() -- so today they
change no register.

⚠ ONE CONSTRAINT ON THE DE DRIVER, because the device tree cannot express it.
SET THE RATE BEFORE ENABLING THE MODULE CLOCK. ccu_nm_ops waits for PLL lock only
inside ccu_nm_set_rate(), so a driver that enables first opens the tap on a PLL
that may not have locked (UM 4.1.4.1 Method 2). mainline's
sun8i_mixer_probe() already has that order
(drivers/gpu/drm/sun4i/sun8i_mixer.c:618-625), and `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-display-engine-clock-gate-reset` gives pll-de
CLK_SET_RATE_UNGATE so the lock poll runs on a PLL that is actually on.

THE PORT GRAPH: ONE EDGE IN, ONE EDGE NAMED AND LEFT OUT
---------------------------------------------------------
mixer0 port@1 -> tcon_tv1 port@0 is in, and it is not decoration:

  - mainline's DE2/DE3/DE33 mixer binding makes `ports` with `port@1` REQUIRED
    (Documentation/devicetree/bindings/display/allwinner,sun8i-a83t-de2-mixer.yaml);
  - sun8i_mixer_of_get_id() takes the output endpoint, follows it, and returns
    the REMOTE endpoint's id as the mixer index -- returning -EINVAL if either is
    missing. A mixer node without this subnode does not probe.
  - it is a real signal path, and the register that performs it is DE2TCON_MUX
    at de-top +0x10. Live 0x0000fff5: disp0's nibble is 0x5 and the other three
    are 0xf, on a board with exactly one display pipe up. ⬜ What the value 0x5
    MEANS is UNKNOWN and this patch does not pretend otherwise -- the A733 has
    four TCONs, so a plain index would make TCON_TV1 = 3. Two readings survive
    (a wider global TCON numbering, or an enable bit plus an index). The graph
    says WHICH TCON; the driver writes the nibble; the nibble's encoding is
    settled by writing a different one on a live link and watching the screen.

Neither endpoint carries a reg, so both ids default to 0 (of_graph_parse_endpoint
leaves them at 0 when the property is absent) and this is mixer 0. A second
display pipe arrives as a second endpoint on the TCON side with reg = <1>.

⚠ The NEXT edge, TCON_TV1 -> the DisplayPort transmitter, is deliberately NOT
here. Its shape depends on whether the DP lands as a drm_bridge or as an encoder
component, and that is explicitly undecided.
Guessing it would put a property in the tree that
nothing reads -- which passes the schema, passes review, passes the checks and is
indistinguishable from a working one until silicon says no, the failure mode
that grepping the driver for each new property is meant to prevent. It is owed by
the patch that settles the question. Note that the two omissions are NOT
symmetric with de-top above: a missing reg window half-works silently, a missing
graph edge fails loudly at probe. Include what fails silently; name what fails
loudly.

WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT IN THIS PATCH
--------------------------------------
A `display-engine` node with `allwinner,pipelines`. If the DE driver reuses
sun4i_drv.c -- whose component master walks that property -- it needs one, and
it is four lines:

    de: display-engine {
            compatible = "allwinner,sun60i-a733-display-engine";
            allwinner,pipelines = <&mixer0>;
    };

It is not here because it describes no hardware. It is a driver-model artifact,
and the driver model is undecided: the two blocks already driven on this path,
sun60i-dp.c and sun60i-tcon-tv.c, are plain platform drivers with no component
master between them. Adding a virtual node to serve one of two possible
architectures is the same mistake as guessing the DP graph edge, and it fails
the loud way -- the platform device simply never exists -- so it costs a DT edit
and not a board cycle. The patch that settles the driver shape adds it, or
deletes this paragraph.

power-domains. CM_DESYS_CFG_REG 0x1B04 reads 0x00020000 = STATUS "Power ON",
MODULE_MODE 0, which is its reset value, and the 6 GiB board read exactly
that with its display path up. On this SoC MODULE_MODE is not the effective gate
for a running block -- the same finding as CM_VO1 on the TCON path and CM_USB2
on USB. Nothing to write, so no phandle. ⚠ If a driver ever does power-gate this
domain it must then treat the per-channel scaler coefficient SRAM as
uninitialised; the prior art reports that gating and re-ungating leaves it
holding random data, and it is visible on the screen.

iommus. The live layer address in the vendor capture is 0xfc000000, and whether
that is a physical address or an IOVA behind IOMMU0/IOMMU1 (0x03900000 /
0x03910000, the memory map of the A733 user manual) is UNKNOWN -- and this tree has no IOMMU node to
point at either way. A wrong `iommus` is worse than none, because sun8i_mixer.c
only checks whether the property is PRESENT. Falsified by a scanout that fetches
nothing from a physically-addressed buffer.

COMPATIBLE
----------
"allwinner,sun60i-a733-de-mixer-0", following "allwinner,sun60i-a733-dp" (`arm64-dts-a733-displayport-transmitter`)
and "allwinner,sun60i-a733-tcon-tv" (`arm64-dts-a733-tcon-tv1-and-the-dp-phy-pad-window`). There is no dt-binding document for
it, exactly as there is none for those two; the binding is owed by whichever
patch first sends one of them upstream.

⚠ NO FALLBACK TO "allwinner,sun50i-h616-de33-mixer-0", and the temptation is
real because that driver is in 6.18.36 and would bind. It must not: the H616 cfg
carries .map = {0, 6, 7, 8} -- one VI channel and three UI -- while this
silicon's seven populated slots are {0,1,2} and {6,7,8,9}. Binding the H616
config to A733 silicon programs the wrong channel base for every layer above the
first. The layouts match; the channel map does not, and the difference is exactly
the "an A523/H616 value is plausible, it builds, and it looks right in a diff"
trap that has caught this tree before.

⚠ CONTRACT WITH THE DE DRIVER, which is being written in parallel and owns
drivers/gpu/drm/. This node is the DT side of that contract: compatible
"allwinner,sun60i-a733-de-mixer-0"; reg-names "layers", "top", "display",
"de-top" with "layers" at index 0; clock-names "bus", "mod"; one unnamed reset;
one interrupt. If the driver settles on different names, the driver wins and
this node changes -- but it changes in one place, deliberately, rather than both
sides guessing.

THE COMMENT THAT MOVED
----------------------
The 18-line comment block describing USB3.1 SuperSpeed and the COMB0 combo-PHY
had drifted away from `usb3: usb@6a00000`, the node it describes: `arm64-dts-a733-displayport-transmitter` and `arm64-dts-a733-tcon-tv1-and-the-dp-phy-pad-window`
each inserted a node between the two, so it ended up introducing the DP
transmitter and then the TCON. This patch moves it back to sit directly above
usb@6a00000 and inserts the display-engine node where the comment used to be.
Pure text move, no property touched -- and it is in this patch rather than left
alone because otherwise this patch is the one that makes a USB3 comment appear
to introduce the display engine. Measured rather than asserted: the dts with
ONLY that hunk applied compiles to a dtb byte-identical to the pre-patch one
(26141 bytes, cmp clean).

VERIFIED
--------
Applies at fuzz 0 as part of the full ordered series against a freshly unpacked
6.18.36. scripts/build.sh compile-check passes and the dtb builds with no new
dtc warning against the pre-patch build (re-run with the dtb deleted first, so
the DTC line is a real compile and not a cached no-op).

The compiled dtb was decompiled and inspected rather than the exit code trusted:
mixer@5100000 carries its compatible, four reg windows in the order
0x5100000/0x180000, 0x5008100/0x40, 0x5280000/0x20000, 0x5008000/0x100, the
matching reg-names, interrupts <0 50 4>, clocks <ccu 73> <ccu 72> = CLK_BUS_DE0
+ CLK_DE0, resets <ccu 27> = RST_BUS_DE0, assigned-clock-parents <ccu 71> =
CLK_DE_PLL3X, and a ports/port@1/endpoint whose phandle resolves to the endpoint
under lcd-controller@5731000's new port@0, and back.

⚠ HARDWARE STATUS. THIS VERSION IS ONLY BUILD-TESTED. An EARLIER version of this node was
HW-proven and it is not this one: on 2026-08-19 a test kernel put main +
`clk-sunxi-ng-a733-display-engine-clock-gate-reset` and `arm64-dts-a733-display-engine-node-and-de-to-tcon-edge` + `drm-a733-display-engine-dram-into-tcon-tv1` onto the 8 GiB board and the display engine scanned a real framebuffer out
of DRAM, through TCON_TV1, over the trained DisplayPort link, onto a monitor --
the first pixels from DRAM this project has produced. That kernel carried the
PERI0_480M cut of this node; `assigned-clock-parents` has since changed to
CLK_DE_PLL3X and `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-display-engine-clock-gate-reset` has since taken ownership of PLL_DE. The clock line is the
one thing about this node that a re-run has to re-establish.

WHAT THAT RUN PROVED ABOUT THIS NODE, and it is most of it, because only one
property changed:
  ✅ THE NODE BINDS AND THE GEOMETRY IS RIGHT. The driver probed against these
     four windows, read the version word 0x05008014 = 0x03520100, wrote
     DE2TCON_MUX, GLOBAL_CTL, GLOBAL_SIZE and GLOBAL_CLK through them, and drove
     a channel and a blender.
  ✅ THE FOURTH WINDOW WAS THE RIGHT CALL, and for a stronger reason than the
     one this header first gave -- see the node comment: with de_top +0x00/+0x04/
     +0x08 at zero the entire mixer datapath is UNWRITABLE, and a raw devmem
     write to 0x05101000 read back as if the address were unoccupied. A node
     with only mainline's three DE33 windows could not have reached those
     registers at all.
  ✅ `iommus` ABSENT WAS CORRECT. The stated falsifier was "a scanout that
     fetches nothing from a physically-addressed buffer"; it fetched, from the
     physical address 0xfe400000, with no IOMMU bound.
  ✅ CLK_BUS_DE0's ahb parent: bus-de0 measured at 200000000 Hz, the AHB rate.
  ✅ RST_BUS_DE0: the block came out of reset.
  ⬜ The `ports` graph edge was NOT exercised -- `drm-a733-display-engine-dram-into-tcon-tv1` is a plain platform driver
     and walks no of_graph. It stays a description of the route, unread.

WHAT WOULD PROVE IT, AS ONE OBSERVABLE. With the DE driver bound: the DE version
word at 0x05008014 reads 0x03520100 -- which requires the reset de-asserted, the
gate passed and a running clock, i.e. it exercises all three of this node's CCU
references at once -- and then, with the driver writing a background colour into
display pipe 0 and clearing TCON_TV1's TV_SRC_CTL bit 0, the monitor stops
showing the VDBIST test pattern and shows that colour, and shows a different one
when the value is changed.

WHAT WOULD FALSIFY IT, in the order the failures separate. Absolute addresses
throughout, because an offset read against the wrong base returns a plausible
number rather than an error:

  - 0x05008014 reads 0x00000000 -> the block is not clocked or not out of reset.
    Read DE0_BGR abs 0x02002A04 (want 0x00010001), DE0_CLK abs 0x02002A00 (want
    0x80000000) and PLL_DE_CTRL abs 0x020022E0 (want bit 31 and bit 28 set). If
    all three are right and the version word is still zero, the base or the
    window is wrong, not the clock tree.
  - /sys/kernel/debug/clk/de0/clk_parent (or clk_summary) shows de0's parent as
    anything but de-pll3x -> this node's assigned-clock-parents did not take.
  - PLL_DE_CTRL still reads 0x41125600 after a modeset -- the cold reset value.
    Nothing reached the PLL: propagation is not happening and only DE0_CLK's own
    divider moved.
  - PLL_DE_CTRL bit 28 (LOCK) clear after a modeset -> the PLL was enabled
    without locking, i.e. the DE driver enabled the module clock before setting
    its rate. That is the one ordering constraint neither this node nor `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-display-engine-clock-gate-reset` can
    enforce, and it is named in the node comment.
  - the version word reads correctly but the screen keeps the VDBIST pattern ->
    the DE is alive and DE2TCON_MUX is wrong, which is the de-top window's own
    test.
  - the screen goes black with TV_DEBUG_REG bit30 TV_FIFO_UNDER_FLOW or bit29
    LOSTWARN set, or its line counter stops -> the DE is driving the TCON and
    starving it. A commit problem, not a description problem: the engine is on
    the same 600 MHz source the vendor drives this mode with.

Assisted-by: Claude

